# Home SOC Lab: Building, Networking, and Monitoring a Windows Security Pipeline

## Overview

This project is a hands-on home Security Operations Center (SOC) lab. I built a multi-VM environment consisting of an attacker machine, a Windows target, and a centralized SIEM manager, configured their networking so they could talk to each other, and successfully captured live security alerts.

## Lab Architecture & Networking

To make this lab work, I set up a local network environment involving three virtual machines:
* **The Attacker:** Kali Linux (used for scanning and attack simulation)
* **The Target:** Windows Virtual Machine (the endpoint being monitored)
* **The Shield (SIEM):** Wazuh Manager (collecting and analyzing logs)

### Networking Troubleshooting & Configuration
Before any monitoring could happen, I had to ensure the VMs could actually talk to each other. This involved:
* Adjusting virtual network adapter settings (bridged/NAT configuration) so the machines shared a reachable subnet.
* Troubleshooting local firewall rules and routing paths to allow traffic between the Kali attacker box, the Windows target, and the Wazuh manager.
* Verifying connectivity using network scanning and local loop diagnostics to make sure agent telemetry packets could reach the manager without getting dropped.

## What I Did (Step-by-Step)

### 1. Fixing Missing Windows Services
When I first installed the monitoring agent on the Windows target, the background service didn't show up in Windows `services.msc`. I used administrative command-line tools (`sc.exe`) to check service status, fix registration issues, and get the agent running smoothly in the background.

### 2. Configuring Event Log Monitoring
I edited the agent's configuration file (`ossec.conf`) to ensure it was actively watching the Windows `Security` event log using `eventchannel`. This told the agent to monitor every login attempt happening on the machine.

### 3. Testing the System
I ran a forced failed login test (triggering Windows Event ID 4625 / error code `1326`). I watched the agent logs (`ossec.log`) to confirm it was successfully shipping the data across the network to the server.

## End Results

After fixing the network routing between the VMs and sorting out the Windows service setup, the telemetry data finally hit the dashboard! I was able to see live authentication failure alerts pop up right on my Wazuh screen, proving that the end-to-end network and monitoring loop was working.

## Skills Developed / Learned

* **Virtual Networking:** Configuring VM network adapters and diagnosing routing/firewall issues across multiple machines.
* **Endpoint Monitoring:** Configuring Windows log channels to track security events.
* **Troubleshooting:** Fixing Windows service registration bugs and debugging background logs.
* **Security Validation:** Simulating authentication failures and verifying they show up on a live dashboard.
